# No digits in names we own

A digit in a name is almost always a version, a duplicate, or a shrug. `page2` does not say what it is — it says someone already had a `page1`. Names we own describe the thing; when two things need distinguishing, the difference has a word for it.

Companion to [be-full-names-only](be-full-names-only.mdc): that rule bans abbreviating a word, this one bans replacing it with a number.

Applies to every identifier this codebase declares — services, repositories, variables, constants, functions, types, file and folder names, database columns, Redis key segments, env vars, and test locals.

## The rule

```ts
// ✗ the digit is doing the work a word should do
const page1 = await inject(…);
const page2 = await inject(…);
function resolveScope2() {}

// ✓ the distinction, stated
const firstPage = await inject(…);
const nextPage = await inject(…);
function resolveFallbackScope() {}
```

## The exemptions, and why each one is not a name we own

| Kept | Why |
| --- | --- |
| `v1` in `/api/v1`, `PUBLIC_API_VERSION_SEGMENT_V1` | the version **is** the identity of the surface; see `api-versioning.util.ts` |
| `S3`, `OAuth2`, `SHA256`, `utf8`, `base64` | the vendor's or standard's own name |
| A third-party export, on the import side only | `import { Thing2 as Thing } from 'vendor'` — the digit stops at the import and app code never sees it |
| `sec-r4-I1`, `audit-#B2` in **comments** | audit-finding references into `docs/`; they are citations, not identifiers |
| Digits in **values** | `LIMIT 100`, `z-[70]`, HTTP `401`, `[a-z0-9]` in a regex — the rule is about what a thing is *called*, not what it *contains* |

## Why this is a rule and not a preference

The rename that produced it was not cosmetic. In core-fe an icon exported as `Building2` was looked up in a table keyed `Building`; the digit is exactly the kind of detail that survives a careless find-and-replace and leaves an `undefined` component at runtime, which is how it was caught. A naming scheme that cannot be renamed safely is a naming scheme that will not be renamed.
